{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|en|invert|ase}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# {{lb|en|enzyme|organic compound}} An [[enzyme]] that [[catalyze]]s the [[hydrolysis]] of [[sucrose]] into [[glucose]] and [[fructose]] ([[invert sugar]]), used by [[bee]]s to produce [[honey]] and in the food industry to soften chocolate.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|author=L. F. J. Woods; S. J. Swinton|chapter=8: Enzymes in the starch and sugar industries|editors=Gregory A. Tucker; L.F.J. Woods|title=Enzymes in Food Processing|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=-4ZnMbryTqkC&pg=PA257&dq=%22invertase%22%7C%22invertases%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=KypzU5P9M4qKkwXEo4C4Aw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22invertase%22%7C%22invertases%22&f=false|page=257
|passage=The costs incurred for the processing of sucrose with '''invertase''' could be considerably reduced with the introduction of immobilised '''invertases''', by adopting technology similar to that developed for glucose isomerase.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1997|author=Karl-Dieter Entian; Hans-Joachim Schüller|chapter=Chapter 13: Genetics of Di- and Trisaccharide Utilization|editors=Friedrich K. Zimmermann; Karl-Dieter Entian|title=Yeast Sugar Metabolism|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=VSQZ1AVg74oC&pg=PA226&dq=%22invertase%22%7C%22invertases%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=KypzU5P9M4qKkwXEo4C4Aw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22invertase%22%7C%22invertases%22&f=false|page=226
|passage=The disaccharide sucrose and the trisaccharide raffinose are both substrates of '''invertase'''. '''Invertase''' is encoded by the ''SUC'' genes from which six unlinked loci have been identified (''SUC1-SUC5'', ''SUC7''), which are dipersed through the yeast genome (Lampen, 1968; Ouwehand and van Wijk, 1972; Ottolenghi, 1971).}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=J. S. C. Clark; P. T. N. Spencer-Phillips|chapter=The Compatible Interaction in Downy Mildew Infections|editors=Peter Spencer-Phillips; Michael J. Jeger|title=Advances in Downy Mildew Research|volume=2|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=T7e6Dj7-jTcC&pg=PA18&dq=%22invertase%22%7C%22invertases%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=KypzU5P9M4qKkwXEo4C4Aw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22invertase%22%7C%22invertases%22&f=false|page=18
|passage=Up-regulation of these '''invertases''' is a common response to infection (Hall and Williams, 2000).}}
#* '''2013''', Maria Kamiko Kadowaki, Rite de Cássia Garcia Simão, Jose Luís da Conceição Silva, Clarice Aoki Osaku, Luis Henrique Souza Guimarāes, ''1: Biotechnical Advances in Fungal '''Invertases''''', Maria de Lourdes T. M. Polizeli, Mahendra Rai (editors), ''Fungal Enzymes'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=RZMAAAAAQBAJ&pg=PA14&dq=%22invertase%22%7C%22invertases%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=KypzU5P9M4qKkwXEo4C4Aw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22invertase%22%7C%22invertases%22&f=false page 14],
#*: The methods used for the immobilization of fungal '''invertases''' include adsorption, covalent binding, cross-linking and entrapment (Uzun et al. 2011).
#*: Nelson and Griffin (1916) reported that '''invertase''' was the first enzyme to be immobilized, and it was bound on charcoal and aluminium hydroxide. Subsequently, mant fungal '''invertases''' have been immobilized onto a variety of media{{...}}.

